<h1>Vibrant Festivals of the Northeast</h1>

<section class="region-overview mb-2">
    <p>Festivals are the lifeblood of Northeast India, painting the region with vibrant colors, rhythmic music, and deep-rooted traditions. They offer a fascinating window into the diverse cultural landscape, marking agricultural cycles, religious events, historical milestones, and community bonding. From the joyous harvest celebrations to solemn religious ceremonies, exploring the festivals is key to understanding the spirit of the Northeast.</p>
    <p>Here's a glimpse into some of the most significant and captivating festivals celebrated across the eight states:</p>
</section>

<hr class="mb-2">

<section class="festivals-showcase">
    <h2>A Tapestry of Celebration</h2>

    <div class="festival-grid">

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Bihu+Festival" alt="Representation of Bihu Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Bihu (Assam)</h3>
                <p>Assam's most important festival, Bihu, is celebrated three times a year (Rongali/Bohag, Kongali/Kati, Bhogali/Magh) marking different agricultural phases. Rongali Bihu, celebrating the Assamese New Year in April, is the most vibrant, featuring joyous folk songs, Bihu dance, traditional feasts, and colourful attire.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Hornbill+Festival" alt="Representation of Hornbill Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Hornbill Festival (Nagaland)</h3>
                <p>Held annually in early December at Kisama Heritage Village, the Hornbill Festival is a spectacular showcase of the rich culture of all Naga tribes. It features traditional music, dances, crafts, indigenous games, food fairs, and elaborate warrior log drums, often called the "Festival of Festivals."</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Sangai+Festival" alt="Representation of Sangai Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Sangai Festival (Manipur)</h3>
                <p>Named after Manipur's state animal, the Sangai deer, this grand festival held in November promotes the state's tourism potential. It showcases Manipuri culture through classical dance performances, indigenous sports like Thang-Ta (martial art), local handicrafts, and diverse cuisines.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Losar+Festival" alt="Representation of Losar Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Losar (Arunachal Pradesh / Sikkim)</h3>
                <p>Celebrated as the Tibetan New Year by communities like the Monpa (Arunachal) and Bhutia/Tibetan communities (Sikkim), Losar typically falls in February/March. Festivities involve prayers at monasteries, traditional dances (like the Aji Lhamu), family gatherings, and hoisting prayer flags.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Chapchar+Kut" alt="Representation of Chapchar Kut Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Chapchar Kut (Mizoram)</h3>
                <p>Mizoram's most popular spring festival, Chapchar Kut, is celebrated in March after the completion of jungle-clearing operations for cultivation. It's marked by community feasts, cultural performances, colourful traditional attire, and the famous Cheraw (bamboo dance).</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Wangala+Festival" alt="Representation of Wangala Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Wangala (Meghalaya)</h3>
                <p>A vibrant post-harvest festival celebrated by the Garo tribe, usually in November. Known as the "100 Drums Festival," it marks the end of the agricultural season and features rhythmic drum beats, traditional dances, feathered headgear, and offerings to the sun god, Misi Saljong.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

         <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Garia+Puja" alt="Representation of Garia Puja">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Garia Puja (Tripura)</h3>
                <p>A major festival for the Tripuri and other tribal communities in Tripura, Garia Puja is held in April to worship Lord Garia, the deity of wealth, livestock, and children. It involves sacrificing fowl, dancing, singing, and seeking blessings for prosperity.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="festival-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Saga+Dawa" alt="Representation of Saga Dawa Festival">
            <div class="festival-card-body">
                <h3>Saga Dawa (Sikkim)</h3>
                <p>An extremely significant Buddhist festival celebrated on the full moon day of the 4th Tibetan lunar month (usually May/June), commemorating Lord Buddha's birth, enlightenment, and death. Monks lead grand processions with holy scriptures from monasteries like Rumtek.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

    </div>
